summary: What the storage work of November 2026 left to look at again — replace and rename as ordinary operations, and compressing what the log keeps of a deletion.

Raised while building the tests of undo and redo ([[The operation log]], `tests/undo_redo_properties.rs`) and the change of the value partition to postings ([[Sorting and postings]]). None is decided.

* ''Replacing a value and renaming a field are not mass writes.'' `PATCH /metarecords/:uuid/fields/:name` and `POST …/fields/:name/rename` write one row at a time (`replace_owned_row`), and so does `retype_field`: a replacement of a value on every match of a query is as many operations as records, in the log and in the store. The batch endpoint (`query/fields/batch`) only runs the four mass writes in one transaction. To see again: whether a replacement (a remove then an add) and a rename (an unset then a set) should be one log entry each, or composed from the mass writes, and what each reads as in the log.
* ''Compressing what the log keeps of a deletion'' (user, 17 November 2026). A deletion keeps the rows of its record in the before of its log entry, in the compact value form; the other effects keep theirs grouped by value (nothing for a remove, the targets that held each value for a set or an unset, [[Log storage]]). A block of consecutive records of a mass deletion, compressed with a general algorithm, may be cheap enough to write the rows when the deletion is made, which would remove the dependence of a pending deletion given back on the state of its records, which its pending `delete` and a copy at the first write settle without writing the rows at the deletion ([[Mass operations in the log]]). Not measured. To see: the ratio on a real repository, the cost of reading one record of a block, and whether the values of `metarecords` and `log_targets` should be compressed whatever the table.
